+# Source and prior-attempt gate: 30003853
+
+Gate completed 2026-10-02 UTC. Rank 408; alias OWR-16167-025. No proof turn is charged for this gate.
+
+## Exact target
+
+Röver–Sapir Question 111, OWR 26/2018, printed page 1625:
+
+> Does every finitely presented subgroup of F have torsion-free abelianisation?
+
+Here F is the usual orientation-preserving dyadic piecewise-linear group of the unit interval, with finitely many breakpoints and slopes integral powers of 2. There is no closedness, solvability, finite-index, or orbit hypothesis in the question. Finite presentation cannot be replaced by finite generation. Source PDF: https://ems.press/content/serial-article-files/46748 ; publisher: https://ems.press/journals/owr/articles/16167 ; DOI 10.4171/OWR/2018/26. Report 15 (2018), no.2, 1579–1633; workshop 3–9 June 2018; actual publication 12 April 2019. Thus the imported 2019 citation is a publication date, not an incorrect workshop year.
+
+The full surrounding discussion and Röver's preceding example on printed 1624–1625 were read; printed 1625 was inspected visually. The known example lies in Z wr Z and is generated by t, a_0^2 and a_0^{-1}a_1. It has 2-torsion in its abelianization. The source does not claim it is finitely presented. The nearby question about embedding Cleary's irrational-slope F_tau in F is distinct. Its known torsion in abelianization is no counterexample without an embedding.
+
+## Retrieval and prior work
+
+The requested https://www.unsolvedmath.com/problems/30003853 was attempted but unavailable through the web tool. The full record in the pinned dataset (revision 37e53eabe540fb458758e198be61634bd02ee008) was read, including its dated 2026-08-21 literature triage. Its research-results entry is null. These imported statements are source metadata, not an author attempt or a proof.
+
+A fetched inventory of 423 repository remote refs found no exact-ID, alias, or Thompson/abelianization target artifact, branch, or commit. All-state PR searches for the ID, alias and both abelianization spellings returned zero target PRs. The current source queue has rank 408 queued, 0/5. Related-target metadata has no exact alias. This does not exclude uncommitted, unreachable or differently indexed work. No substantive campaign attempt was recovered.
+
+An earlier imported desk suggestion proposed diagram subgroups as possible counterexamples. This route is mathematically unavailable: Guba–Sapir's Theorem 9.9 says every integral homology group of every diagram group is free abelian. This applies to H_1, without any finiteness hypothesis. That imported suggestion is corrected here rather than adopted.
+
+## Primary literature checked, with scope
+
+- Guba–Sapir, *Diagram groups and directed 2-complexes: homotopy and homology*, arXiv:math/0301225v2 (10 May 2003), Theorem 9.9, PDF p35; published JPAA 205 (2006), 1–47, DOI 10.1016/j.jpaa.2005.06.012. The exact theorem and its retraction proof were read and the page inspected visually. https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0301225
+- Bieri–Geoghegan–Kochloukova, *The Sigma invariants of Thompson's group F*, arXiv:0807.5138v1, Theorem 2.7, PDF p6 (visually inspected), and coefficient-ring convention p2. For any nonzero commutative coefficient ring, a group of type FP_2 with no nonabelian free subgroup splits as an ascending HNN extension over a finitely generated subgroup of the kernel of any nonzero discrete character. They explicitly credit Bieri–Strebel, CMH 53 (1978), Theorem A. Published GGD 4 (2010), 263–273. https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.5138
+- Golan, *Finiteness properties of closed subgroups of Thompson's group F*, arXiv:2609.14702v1, 13 September 2026. Theorem 2.13 identifies closed subgroups as diagram groups, citing earlier work. The finiteness algorithms in Theorem 8.3 and Corollary 8.7 have finite/full-core hypotheses (equivalently in the stated setting, finitely many dyadic orbits); those are not removed. The abstract, introductory qualifications and these relevant statements/proofs were read. This does not resolve arbitrary finitely presented subgroups. https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.14702
+- Farley, *Diagram groups and groups of piecewise linear homeomorphisms of the line with global fixed points*, arXiv:2606.27753v2, posted 11 July 2026 (PDF date 14 July). Theorem 1.2 and Corollary 1.3 concern the full groups fixing a specified finite sequence, not all their subgroups. They give further known positive classes, and explicitly acknowledge earlier overlap with Golan-Polak–Sapir. https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.27753
+- Burillo–Nucinkis–Reeves, *An irrational-slope Thompson's group*, Publ. Mat.65 (2021), 809–839, DOI 10.5565/PUBLMAT6522112. The primary PDF's concluding Question 10.1 repeats this finitely presented-subgroup problem, with the embedding question separate. https://mat.uab.cat/pubmat/articles/view_doi/10.5565/PUBLMAT6522112
+
+The original Bieri–Strebel archive download returned a verification HTML page, not a PDF; no challenge was solved. The available primary BGK statement is the dependency actually used. Source PDFs/images are local-only; public artifacts provide links and hashes, not redistributed source files.
+
+## Gate result
+
+Eligible for scoped author work. No complete later solution was verified in the targeted current primary-source search. This is not a claim of an exhaustive literature census. The original remains unresolved. No historical novelty is claimed for the elementary consequences developed here.
